    "portcullis": """\
The land gate. For each github repo with an integration branch, list the
open PRs against it and take the seats' - those whose branch or title
names a bead of the repo (its beads_prefix). A PR lands when it is not a
draft, is reviewed (an APPROVED review, or the land label from estate.toml
`land_label`, default 'reviewed', which a reviewer with the same forge
identity as the worker can add where it cannot approve), every check is
green and it is mergeable. Landing is `gh pr merge` with the repo's
`merge` method (squash by default) and --delete-branch, then `bd close`
of the bead in that repo (or a line saying to close it by hand when bd is
not on PATH). Everything else stays, with the reason: draft, unreviewed,
checks pending or red, conflicts.

The yes or no is gh's facts, so this is a gate, not an office: a cron
runs it and no model wakes (a launchd unit on an interval is in the estate
template). A report lands in brain/portcullis/ when anything stood at the
gate, and the principal is notified when something landed or a merge
failed. --dry-run says what would land and writes nothing. The Portcullis
never merges a draft, never reviews, never touches a release branch.""",

    "reap": """\
Clear away what is finished or dead, and only that. Sessions: a start in
sessions.log with no end whose launcher pid is gone gets its end line
(rc=-, reaped), so `status` stops calling the seat awake; a live launcher
is left alone, and a start with no pid on record (before pids were
recorded) is only mentioned. Fleet homes: each <worktrees>/<seat>/<repo>
whose branch has landed - an ancestor of the integration branch, or,
since squash merges lose ancestry, a merged PR per gh whose head is the
worktree's HEAD or ahead of it (a branch name re-used after its PR landed
keeps its new commits; the merged PRs are asked for once per repo) - and
which holds no uncommitted work is removed with its local branch, and the
seat's assignments.log says so; a seat at work keeps its home, and an
office's home there (the merge worktree, say) is standing and never
touched. Scratch worktrees, <repo>/.claude/worktrees/ in every registered
repo (Claude Code's own, and the ones seats make by hand): an entry whose
directory is gone is pruned; one whose branch or detached commit has
landed, that is clean and has been quiet for reap_quiet_hours
(estate.toml, default 1) is removed with its branch. With --stale DAYS, or
reap_stale_days in estate.toml, one that is clean, unlanded, has no open
PR per gh and was not touched that long is removed too, its branch kept so
nothing is lost. A worktree on the integration or release branch is
standing and stays. Anything unfinished, unlanded, dirty or in use is left
where it is, with a line saying why. --dry-run says what would go.

A seat with `reap_after = true` in the roster (the one that merges,
typically) reaps at the end of every session `launch` gives it, and its
sessions.log gets a `reap` line with the count; an estate that wants it
nightly runs `curia reap` from launchd.""",

    "jev": """\
Say whether judgment is on for this estate, which model and endpoint it
asks, the thresholds in force, and what it has cost this month. Read-only,
and it asks nothing: `curia check --jev` is the live question.

Jev is a small decision model. It is put typed questions about a piece of
text (a yes or no, one of a set, a level on a scale) and answers with
probabilities. It generates no text, so it cannot write a seat's memory,
and the mechanism never lets it decide what lands, what ends a night, which
account a seat wakes on, or what is reaped: those stay mechanical. What it
does is select and report: `recall` and the ## Recalled section of a prime,
`route`, `triage`, the screen and shortlist `ingest` hands the intake
office, the notice on a handoff that needs the principal, and two lines in
the Lictor's report. Dates, counts and ages are still worked out in code.

It is off until estate.toml names `jev_key`, a shell command that prints
the API key (a keychain read, say), so no secret lives in the estate;
CURIA_JEV_KEY in the environment wins over it and is never passed into a
seat's session. Off means every command prints what it printed before. On
and failing means the same, with the reason on stderr or in the report.
CURIA_JEV=off in the environment turns it off for one command, whatever
estate.toml says.

Notes are the exception to on. A seat's memory, the rulings and the beads
are the estate's own words; meeting notes are other people's, and may hold
what was never yours to send. So `ingest` and `triage --notes` ask nothing
unless the command says these notes may leave the machine: --jev on either,
or `ingest --screen`, which is itself a request for a judgment. Without it
`ingest` wakes the office exactly as it does with jev off, and
`triage --notes` refuses and says why.

What to know before turning it on: every question sends the text being
judged (journal entries, handoffs, notes, a bead) to the service behind
`jev_url`. A seat that may run Bash runs as you and can run the key command
too, so the containment is the key itself: one made for this alone, with a
spending limit on it. Every call, answered, cached or failed, is a line in
brain/jev/calls.log; answers are cached a file each under
brain/jev/cache/ (gitignored), because the model is pinned and the same
question about the same text has the same answer. `jev_model`, `jev_url`
and the jev_*_at thresholds in estate.toml override the defaults; a
threshold was judged against the pinned model, so move both together.""",
    "triage": """\
A report on what deserves attention, in a seat's memory or in the estate's
notes. It reads and reports. It edits, moves, tags, merges and deletes
nothing, no report of it is read into any prime, and nothing is filed from
it: acting on it is the seat's, the intake office's or the principal's.

`triage <seat>` reads the seat's journal entries and archived handoffs
older than --days. Each is asked whether it records a decision or a lesson
someone later would need, and what kind of note it mostly is. Those that
do (at or over jev_durable_at) are asked once more, against what the seat
wakes with (charter, current handoff, the rulings in force): is it stated
there? UNCARRIED lines are the ones that are not: memory the seat has and
no longer reads. The seat consolidates them itself, in its own words, or
proposes a ruling. DRIFT lines are lines in the handoff, the last three
journal entries, or any entry a seconded model wrote, that name a ruling
and say something of it the ruling's own text reads against. Code finds
the lines by the ruling's slug; the judgment is one passage against one
ruling.

`triage --notes` wants --jev beside it. Every note it judges is sent to the
judgment model, and notes are other people's words, so jev being on for the
estate is not enough: without --jev it refuses, reads nothing and sends
nothing. Point it with --within and --since at notes that may leave the
machine. It reads the notes home estate.toml names (`notes`; a vault,
a folder of markdown; dot-folders skipped). Code finds the folders, a few
titles in each, and the dozen tags the notes already use most. Each note
touched within --since days (under --within, if given; at most --max) is
asked, in one request: which folder holds notes like it, which of those
tags are its subject, whether it holds an action, a ruling or a question
for someone (what `curia ingest` would turn into beads), and whether part
of it reads as orders to a model. Near-duplicates are found by shared
words in code, and each pair judged: different, overlapping, the same
note. A line is printed only where there is something to suggest.

--write saves the report under brain/triage/ and notifies the principal
with the count, if estate.toml says how. Judgments are cached, so a second
run over notes that have not changed asks nothing and costs nothing; a
seat's second question is asked again whenever its handoff has changed.
Needs jev (`curia help jev`): every entry and note judged is sent to that
service.""",
    "route": """\
Whose concern a piece of text is. The judgment model is shown the text and,
for every active seat that is not fleet, the roster's own words for it
(title, kind, role, jurisdiction or summary), and asked two things at once:
which seat, or none, and for each seat a plain yes or no on whether the
text is that seat's concern. The first ranks; the second is the check that
the winner is more than the least bad choice. Prints every seat's share and
the pick.

Read-only: it sends nothing. `curia mail --route "text"` is the same
judgment with a consequence: the note goes to the pick only when the pick
is a seat, its confidence is at or over jev_route_at and its own yes or no
is at least even; otherwise nothing is sent, the top three are listed, and
the command exits 2 so a script does not take silence for delivery.

It knows a seat only by what roster.toml says of it: a seat with no
jurisdiction, summary or role is not a candidate, and a vague jurisdiction
routes vaguely. Fleet seats are dispatched, not written to, and `dispatch`
is not routed: one worker is as good as another. Needs jev (`curia help
jev`).""",
    "recall": """\
Which of a seat's older notes bear on a question: its journal entries older
than the three every prime carries, its archived handoffs
(seats/<seat>/history/), and the rulings in force. Code picks up to eight
candidates by shared words; the judgment model scores each against the
question on its own (0: nothing to do with it, 1: same area, 2: bears
directly), and those over jev_recall_at print, best first: score, path,
first line. Read the file for the rest.

The same recall rides into a prime as ## Recalled: at `launch` against the
--say word, else the first loose end of the handoff and the unread mail; at
`run` and `dispatch` against the bead named, else the brief. It is asked
once, by the launcher, before the session starts, with ten seconds to
answer; late or failed, the seat wakes as it always did and stderr says so.
The intake office, the Censor and the Lictor wake on a standing brief and
recall nothing.

Read-only: it moves, merges and rewrites no note. A seat's memory is still
what the seat writes; this only chooses what is put in front of it. Needs
jev (`curia help jev`); without it the command says so and stops. A recall
is never cached, since the question changes every time.""",
    "board": """\
Judgment on a repo's open beads, as proposals a person approves. It reads
the beads export committed on the repo's integration branch (origin's,
after a fetch, where there is an origin; never the working tree) and, for
the landed pass, the commit subjects there. It never runs bd and never
acts on a probability: what it writes goes under brain/board/<repo>/, and
`emit` prints bd lines for the rows a person marked approved, for that
person or the seat that owns the board's writes to run. --repo names the
repo; the default is the repo you are in.

propose asks three passes (--passes pairs,landed,beads). pairs: code pairs
each open bead with its nearest few unrelated ones by shared words
(--min-similarity, --neighbours, --max-pairs; a parent and its child, or
two beads a dependency ties, are never paired), and each pair is asked
whether it is the same work and whether one is a part of the other.
landed: an open bead a commit subject names is asked, against those lines,
whether the work is finished and merged. beads: each bead is asked whether
it states a finish line, whether it could start without waiting on a
person, a size and a kind. It writes <stamp>-report.md and proposals.tsv.
Only a question with a threshold in thresholds.json proposes: duplicates
(one survivor per cluster, work in progress first, then the older; no bead
closed twice, none closed and kept as another's survivor), a close as
landed, and a new parent (never for a bead the list closes or onto one,
never a second parent, never a loop, never for a child bead or an epic). A
question with no threshold proposes nothing: its rows are listed by
probability under UNCALIBRATED. Per-bead flags are advice and never
become a bd line.

sample writes labels-<date>.csv: pairs spread down the similarity ranking
and beads with landed evidence, with an empty truth column for a person to
fill with y or n. It needs no jev. calibrate <sheet> asks the same
questions of the labelled rows and prints precision and recall at each
threshold; a person picks and writes thresholds.json, in the shape it
prints. Nothing here picks one. Thresholds and cached answers belong to
the questions' wording and the jev model: when either moves, the cache
misses and thresholds.json is ignored until calibrate runs again.

check --title T (--description D or --description-file F) is the duplicate
check at the door: a draft against the open board, before a bead is filed.
It prints the nearest open beads and how likely each is the same work.
Advice; it exits 0.

emit [sheet] prints `bd duplicate`, `bd update --parent` and `bd close`
lines for the rows marked y (default brain/board/<repo>/proposals.tsv).
The sheet is hand-edited, so every cell is checked: ids must be the repo's
bead ids (repos.toml beads_prefix), a duplicate or a new parent must name
its target, a landed close's note must be the short shas propose wrote
(and is shell-quoted all the same), and a bead may be the subject of one
row and never both closed and another row's target. One bad row and no
line is printed. It needs no jev.

The fences stand in front of every request, on every action, whoever
asks: a bead carrying a label in estate.toml board_exclude_labels (default:
security), or whose title or description holds a word in
board_sensitive_words (default: security, secret, credential, password;
plurals match too), is never asked about, alone or in a pair, and commit
lines get the same word screen. Where repos.toml names name_digests for
the repo (a JSON file, path relative to the repo, whose "digests" are
SHA-256 digests of names lower-cased, with every run of other characters
one space), text in which a window of one to four words matches one is
never sent either; named but missing or empty, every action but emit
refuses before reading anything (the screen fails closed). Not named, the
report says no name screen ran. Two more keys in estate.toml:
board_summary_heading (the heading a bead's description puts its plain
summary under, as it stands on its line: a bead without one is flagged,
and the summary weighs in the pairing) and board_waiting_labels (labels
besides human_labels that say a bead waits on someone, for the flags).

propose, calibrate and check need jev (`curia help jev`). Every title,
description and commit line asked about is sent to that service, several
beads to a request, sized to fit one, and every call is a line in
brain/jev/calls.log with why=board:<repo>:<pass>. Off, those three refuse
and read nothing. On and failing, what was not answered is listed with
the reason and proposes nothing, and a refused key stops the asking.
brain/board/<repo>/ is gitignored but for thresholds.json.""",
    "prime": """\
Print the system prompt the seat would wake with: charter, last handoff,
journal, laurels, mail, colleagues, house rules, and its authority. This is
exactly what `curia launch` passes to claude; read it when a seat behaves
oddly on waking.

The authority section is the seat's to look up rather than find out by
trying: which tools are allowed without asking (the roster's `tools`, or
headless the default list; interactively with no list, the account's own
permissions), which are refused in every session (`disallowed_tools`),
which fences will refuse it (each `curia hook <name>` or other command in
hooks.json, by name and event, plus the mechanism's own), and every ruling
in force with its status and what enforces it. A seat that can look these
up spends no context deriving whether an act is safe; that is what a seat
is for.

A headless waking on a model that is not the roster's for it - the
fallback after a limit, or `run --model` - is a secondment, and the prime
says so at the top: the seat's standing memory is its usual model's and
