    "launch": """\
Wake a crew seat or office interactively. Resolves the account (walking the
fallback chain past any limited account), links the mechanism's skills into
that account's config dir, builds the prime, and runs claude with CURIA_SEAT
set in the repo you launched from - the registered repo whose path contains
the working directory, the most specific one where repos nest - or, from
outside any repo, in the seat's roster home. --repo names one from anywhere.

Which estate the seat belongs to is settled first, before the account or
the directory: --estate, CURIA_ESTATE, a walk up from the working
directory to the nearest curia/estate.toml, then the registry by the
seat's name. Inside a workspace the walk-up wins, so two estates may each
have a seat of the same name and a launch from inside either is
unambiguous. From outside any workspace that shared name stops the
command rather than guess; --estate, CURIA_ESTATE, or a naming family of
the estate's own settles it. The session carries CURIA_ESTATE and
CURIA_SEAT, so every curia command the seat runs inside it - dispatch,
mail, handoff - binds to its estate whatever directory it has moved to.
--print-cmd shows the estate, the account, the seat and the directory a
launch would use, and launches nothing.

Fleet seats and parked seats refuse to launch; fleet is dispatched by its
office, and a parked seat (active = false in the roster) is kept with its
memory but woken by nothing - launch, run, dispatch and a broadcast mail
all pass it by - because each seat costs its wakings, and a roster shrinks
by parking before it shrinks by deleting. --say gives the seat an opening
message - the night's orders - so a looped launch needs no typing; without
it the seat wakes on its prime alone and waits for you.

Account rotation happens here, at launch - a running session never changes
account. The chain is walked in order and the first account with headroom
is taken: not marked limited by `curia limit`, not measured past
limited_at, and under rotate_at on its fullest rate-limit window as its
status line last read it (thresholds in estate.toml; `curia help init`).
Past rotate_at the walk moves on; when every open account is past it, the
emptiest is taken. A reading past limited_at counts as limited until that
window resets, marker or none. An account with `shared = false` in
accounts.toml is only ever its own seats': never a fallback for anyone
else, so the seats that need it always find it. With no reading on file
the walk is markers only, as before. --account starts the walk somewhere
other than the seat's usual account; it is a starting point, not a pin.

Mid-session, the seat's own hooks keep the night alive (`curia help
hook`). Its status line records what Claude Code measures. Its Stop hook,
past handoff_at or past shift_hours awake, tells it once to hand off while
it still has the tokens to write the note; the --loop relaunch then lands
on an account with headroom. Its StopFailure hook, when a turn dies on
the limit, marks the account and leaves a LIMITED marker naming the
session; the launcher, watching for a marker about its own session (a
headless run of the same seat leaves one named for that run, which is not
this launcher's), ends the session (nothing useful can happen there
without tokens) and relaunches on the fallback with a prime line saying
the last session was cut without a handoff and where its transcript is.
Without --loop the same note is printed and the launch ends. When every
account in the chain is limited, a --loop launcher says so once, then
waits in slices of fifteen minutes, re-walking the chain after each, so a
limit cleared by hand or a window that resets early is taken up within
the slice.

--everything, with a repo: work its board until it is clear. Each waking
gets orders naming what is ready, in progress, blocked by other beads on
the board, and waiting on a human - a label from estate.toml
`human_labels`, a blocked bead with nothing open on the board blocking
it, or a bead blocked only by such beads or by ids not on the board; those
are left alone. --loop is implied. After each handoff the board is read
again from the repo's beads export as pushed to origin's integration
branch - the checkout the estate names may be one nobody pulls - or from
the file on disk where the repo has no origin (so the orders tell the seat
to export and push before handing off). The loop ends when nothing remains
that is not waiting on a human, or when two wakings in a row leave the
board as it was - no open bead changed status or was touched, nothing
closed; a bead taken from ready to in progress is movement - so a stuck
seat does not spin all night; the principal is told either way. --say is
appended to the orders.

When a session ends on its handoff, the launcher prints the note back
into the terminal - claude clears the screen on exit, and a launch that
ended in silence left the principal with an empty terminal and a note to
go and find (2026-09-18). The block is the seat, session number, minutes
and cost, the whole "For the principal" section, the first item under
"Loose ends and what next", and the note's path; it prints before a
--loop relaunch too, so a night leaves a trail per waking above the next
session. A session that ends without a handoff says so instead, with the
date of the note still on file, so an empty terminal never reads as fine.

The record of a night: each waking's reading of the board is a `board`
line in sessions.log (ready, in progress, blocked, waiting on a human,
closed), and each handoff is reported to the principal through `notify`
in estate.toml - what the session took, how the board moved under it from
the reading before to the reading after (beads closed, beads filed), how
much waits on a person, and the first line under the handoff's "For the
principal". `curia progress <seat>` reads all of this back, waking by
waking, with the distance to the loop's end.

A waking whose fetch of origin failed reads the last board again and is
not counted as idle; the launcher says so, and the fetch is bounded (one
branch, a minute) so a dead network costs a minute, not the night. A
session that ends without a handoff is woken again while the board says
so, unless it died at once. Given --repo more than once, the boards are
worked in that order: when one is clear or has stalled, the seat moves on
to the next and its sessions start there; the loop ends after the last,
and each board's outcome is reported on its own. A session that dies at
once ends the whole night, not just that board.

--until and --budget cap the night: a deadline and a spend limit. Both require
--loop or --everything. --until takes HH:MM (the next occurrence, local time)
or an ISO datetime (UTC assumed if naive); the launcher reads it as UTC and
prints both forms, e.g. "06:30 local (01:00Z)", because you type local and the
record is UTC. An ISO time already past is an error. The seat is told to wrap
up wrap_minutes before (default 45 minutes, estate.toml), so long turns and
gates finish in time. --budget, in US dollars, is the recorded spend across
this night's sessions; the seat is told to wrap at budget_wrap_at percent
(default 90%). The launcher tracks spend, told in the environment (CURIA_SPENT),
across the wakings that reported it. Neither ends the night mid-turn: the shift
hook is what tells the seat to wrap; these set its alarm. The night ends across
all boards (with several --repo) without moving to the next; the record says so.
One short last waking that starts just before the margin is possible and a true
record, not a fault. This is not a hard stop: a turn in flight finishes, but the
margin makes the deadline hold in practice.

The record: each session gets a fresh session id, written to sessions.log
with its start and end, so a transcript can be found again (claude
--resume <id> under that account). The end line carries the cost and
minutes the status line last reported for the session, so `status` can
total interactive spend. The prime goes to claude in a temporary file, not
on the command line. A RESTART marker left by an earlier handoff is
cleared at launch: this launch is the waking it asked for.

--loop relaunches after each /handoff. When every account in the chain is
limited, a looped launch does not die: it logs a wait, notifies if the
estate says how, sleeps until the earliest reset time and tries again.
Without --loop, a spent chain is an error, since you are at the keyboard.

The session carries Claude Code hooks, passed with --settings so no
account's settings.json is edited: the mechanism's, which refuses a manual
/compact with a reminder to /handoff instead (`curia help hook` says how
the principal allows one), then whatever fences the estate declares in
curia/hooks.json. Auto-compaction is not blocked.

The authority envelope comes from the roster: `disallowed_tools` are
refused for the whole session, in every session, and that is the fence;
`tools`, when the roster names a list, are allowed without asking.
Interactively, a seat with no `tools` list gets whatever the account's own
permissions say. A roster `effort` (low, medium, high, xhigh, max) is
passed as --effort, so a reviewer pool can run low and an office high
without any account's settings being touched. The prime states the whole
envelope, the fences and the rulings in force under "Your authority", so
the seat looks them up instead of finding out (`curia help prime`). The
start line in sessions.log records the model and the
launcher's pid, so after an upgrade the seat's history says which model it
was, and `reap` can tell a dead launcher from a live one.""",

    "run": """\
Run a seat headless: invoke `claude -p` with the seat's prime as system
prompt and a prompt file as the message. Any seat, not only an office. This
is how an office wakes a crew seat: a subagent spawned inside a session
inherits that session's account and model, whereas `run` resolves the
seat's OWN account from the roster (walking its fallback chain), links the
skills there, and gives the seat its own prime - charter, last handoff,
laurels, journal. It runs in the repo
you are in, or the one --repo names, or else the estate root. The prompt is
looked up in the estate's prompts/ directory unless given as a path; {placeholders}
in it are filled from the estate, the --repo entry in repos.toml, and --set
pairs (unknown ones, and any other braces, are left intact). Output is
printed and logged under brain/<seat>/, and the seat's sessions.log records
a headless waking. Without --skip-permissions the seat gets the tool list
from its roster entry, or Bash, Read, Edit, Write, Glob, Grep and Agent.

The model is the seat's `run_model` from the roster when set, else its
`model`: a seat can talk to the principal on one model and be woken
headless on a cheaper one. --model overrides both for this run only; the
roster is the record, so a standing change goes there, not in a flag. A
run on any model other than the roster's for it - --model, or the
fallback after a limit - is a secondment: the prime says so, the seat is
told to leave its standing memory (charter, laurels, handoff) as its usual
model left it, and the journal entry opens with `seconded: <model>` so the
next waking knows whose notes it reads; the start line in sessions.log
says `seconded` too. A roster `effort` rides along as --effort. A parked
seat (active = false) is not run. --print-cmd shows the resolved account,
model, cwd and prompt size and runs nothing.

The run is a process of its own. The mechanism forks it free of the shell
that asked for it - a new session, reparented to init, stdout and stderr to
seats/<seat>/runs/<stamp>-<label>.log - because that shell is often a seat
session that hands off and ends before an hour-long run returns, and a
plain child of it died with it. By default the caller waits and prints the
log when the run ends, so a foreground call reads as before, and Ctrl-C
stops only the waiting; --detach prints the pid and the log path and
returns at once. Either way the record (sessions.log, brain/<seat>/, and
assignments.log when a bead is named) is the run's own and is complete
whether or not the caller is still there. A run cut at --timeout is ended
with its whole process group, so a server the seat started does not go on
holding its port after the seat is gone.

Memory: there is no /handoff in a headless run, so the prompt ends with
orders to write a short journal entry to seats/<seat>/journal/<stamp>-<repo>.md
before answering, and the prime carries the seat's last three entries. One
file per run, so overlapping runs of one seat never race. The mechanism
never writes the entry; if the seat did not, sessions.log says
journal=missing and stderr says so.

The record: the run uses claude's JSON output, so sessions.log gets the
session id, cost, turns and duration alongside rc, and the answer under
brain/<seat>/ ends with the same line. The session id is chosen here and
passed to claude, so the start line carries it too (with the launcher's
pid), and `status` can total the spend by account and seat. Only the seat's
answer is printed. The exit status is non-zero when the run failed.

The run carries the same hooks and authority envelope as a launch: the
mechanism's hooks, the estate's hooks.json, `--allowedTools` from the
roster's `tools` (or the default list) and `--disallowedTools` from
`disallowed_tools`. Print mode has no status line, and the run sets
CURIA_HEADLESS, so the shift hook says nothing; the limited hook still
marks the account when a turn dies on the limit.

Limits: a run that dies on a usage limit marks its account limited (until
the reset time the banner names, else for a few hours), then goes once
more down the fallback chain. Each headless run is a launch, and fallback
happens at launch. When the chain is spent, the estate is notified if it
says how, and nothing retries.

A roster `fallback_model` comes before that: when a run dies on a limit,
the same account is tried once more on the fallback model before the
account is marked, since a limit may be the model's rather than the
account's. Both dead means the account is, and the walk continues down
the chain on the primary model. The end line in sessions.log says
fallback_model=<model> when the answer came from it.""",

    "handoff": """\
With no flag, print the seat's current handoff note - the note the next
session wakes up to. --begin and --done belong to the /handoff skill:
