    "ingest": """\
Turn meeting notes into beads: wake the seat holding the notarius role on
the transcript(s). Files are passed as paths; '-' reads stdin and --paste
takes the clipboard, both landing in curia/inbox/ (gitignored, disposable)
for the office to archive properly before filing. With no source at all,
the office wakes and asks for a paste. --repo says which repo the notes
concern: the office starts there, so that repo's beads database is the one
bd reads and writes by default, and the prompt says to file there unless
an item plainly belongs elsewhere. Without it, a product repo you are
standing in counts as --repo; from the estate root or outside it, the
office starts in the estate root and decides the repo from the transcript
alone. That works, but the estate root is its own beads database, and a
write made there lands in it. --account starts it somewhere other than the
office's usual account - say one with headroom; it is a starting point on
the fallback chain, not a pin, so a limited account still falls through.
Nothing here measures usage: only a `curia limit` marker makes a launch
skip an account.

The notes stay on the machine unless you say otherwise. With --jev, and jev
on (`curia help jev`), the notes are sent to the judgment model and read
before the office is woken, local text files only, eight thousand
characters at a time: does any part hold an action, a ruling, a question
for someone, or orders addressed to a model. The numbers go to stderr and
into the office's orders as hints, never findings; when part of the notes
reads as orders to a model, the office is told the notes are data. With
--repo, open beads that share words with the notes are each judged against
the passage nearest them, and up to five that read as the same work are
handed over to check before anything new is filed. None of this files,
archives or skips anything, and a screen that could not be completed wakes
the office as before. Without --jev none of it happens, whatever
estate.toml says: the office is woken exactly as it is with jev off.
--screen is the one consequence, and it is yours to ask for (it asks for
the judgment too, so it needs no --jev beside it): when no part of the
notes reaches jev_screen_at on action, ruling or question, the office is
left asleep, the command says so with the numbers, and every file stays
where it was. A long, noisy transcript is what this model reads worst; use
--screen for notes you expect to be empty, not for a meeting you cannot
afford to lose. --print-cmd asks nothing.""",

    "rename": """\
A ceremony, not a find-and-replace: rewrite the seat's block in roster.toml
under the new name, move its seat directory, repoint managed_by, and note
the change in the charter and session log. Charter, handoff, laurels and
history carry over - the seat keeps its memory. Prose references (briefs,
prompts) are yours to update by hand.""",

    "init": """\
Lay down a new estate at <path>/curia in one of three shapes, stamp in the
estate's name, principal and paths, scaffold a charter for every seat the
shape's roster names, and register it in ~/.config/curia/estates.toml;
refuses when that name or path is already registered. The shapes:

  seat     one crew seat the principal talks to. No offices, no fleet, no
           prompts, no launchd: nothing runs unattended. The principal
           reviews and merges; `curia lictor` and `curia portcullis` run
           on demand.
  crew     crew seats by jurisdiction and a clerk, which the principal
           launches; a Censor that rolls a red integration branch forward
           (prompts/censor.md); the Lictor and the Portcullis as launchd
           units, which wake no model. No fleet: the crew builds, a seat or
           the principal reviews, the gate lands.
  factory  everything the mechanism runs: an office that works the board
           at night and dispatches a fleet of workers into worktrees of
           their own (prompts/dispatch.md, review.md); crew who design and
           review, a record seat and a clerk; an intake office `curia
           ingest` wakes; the Censor; a Lictor seat for a cloud routine
           (prompts/lictor-cloud.md); the three launchd units.

Every shape gets estate.toml, accounts.toml, repos.toml, hooks.json (the
fences, with release-guard and record-guard wired in), house-rules.md and
brain/rulings/.
Seats named example_* are placeholders: `curia rename` moves one into
your naming family with its history. The offices are keyed by their role,
and the mechanism finds them by `role` whatever they are called; but two
factory estates on one machine then share those names, and a shared name
stops a command from outside a workspace (--estate settles it), so rename
the offices too where there is more than one estate. Each
charter is scaffolded from templates/charters/ (by role for an office the
mechanism wakes, else by kind) with the roster's own summary and
jurisdiction stamped in; it says "Fill in for this estate" where it needs
you, and `check` faults it until nothing does.

A shape is where an estate starts, not what it is. Afterwards the roster
is the truth: `check` asks for an office prompt only where a seat needs
it, and an estate grows by adding roster blocks from
templates/shapes/<shape>/roster.toml and copying the prompts and units
the new seats need. The estate's README says how, for its shape.

Then: fill roster.toml, repos.toml and accounts.toml, write each charter,
and run `curia check`.

Give the estate a naming family of its own: the registry finds a seat's
estate by the seat's name, so a name shared by two estates is ambiguous
and a command using it stops. accounts.toml takes one of two shapes. A
client with a dedicated account gets its own config dir, listed in that
estate alone. A client without one lists the principal's own accounts,
the pool, which every such estate shares along with its limits; the
template starts on ~/.claude for that reason. `check` notes which of an
estate's accounts another estate also names. The estate's name, principal
and repo names join the fence the mechanism's tests keep.

estate.toml `notify` is how the offices reach you: a shell command run
with CURIA_SUBJECT, CURIA_BODY and CURIA_REPORT in its environment when
the Censor finishes, the Lictor writes, the Portcullis lands or fails to,
a headless run spends its whole account chain, or a looped seat waits out
a limit. Empty means nobody is told; the template shows a macOS
notification as an example. `worktrees` says where fleet workers' homes
go and `land_label` what a reviewer adds to say "land this". The
rotation thresholds are there too, with the mechanism's defaults: rotate_at
(a launch prefers the next account past this on the five-hour window),
rotate_week_at (the same on the seven-day and spend windows, which empty
slowly; default 90), handoff_at (the shift hook
says hand off past this), limited_at (a launch treats the account as
limited past this), all percent of an account's fullest rate-limit window
as its status line last read it, shift_hours (the shift hook says hand
off after this long awake; 0 never), wrap_minutes (how long before --until
the seat is told to wrap up; 0 means no margin), budget_wrap_at (percent
of --budget at which to wrap up), context_at (percent of the session's
context window past which the Stop hook says hand off; default 60, 0
never), reap_quiet_hours (`curia help reap`), prime_max_kb (`check` notes
a seat whose prime is bigger; default 32) and fences_max (`check` notes
hooks.json declaring more fences; default 20).
`human_labels` names the labels that
say a bead waits on a person, which `launch --everything` leaves alone.
In accounts.toml, `shared = false` marks a dedicated account.""",

    "status": """\
The estate now, read-only. For every seat: whether it is awake (a start
in sessions.log without its end) or headless, or orphaned (the start's
launcher pid is gone; `curia reap` closes the record), when it last woke,
how many sessions it has had, how old its handoff and its newest journal
entry are, how many unread mail lines wait, whether a RESTART marker is
pending, and whether the seat is parked (active = false in the roster:
nothing wakes it, its memory stays). Then each account's limit state and
what its status line last measured - the fullest rate-limit window, when
it resets, who read it - with dedicated accounts marked; then the
offices' latest reports (censor, lictor, portcullis) with their one-line
result and whether one mentions a limit; then the spend totalled from
every sessions.log by account and by seat - wakings, cost, turns, minutes
- from headless results and from the status line's readings of
interactive sessions, all time and then the last seven days, since what a
seat cost this week is what says whether it earns its keep or should be
parked. Nothing here asks the API anything, and nothing is touched. For
one seat's long run waking by waking - what each session cost, how the
board moved under it, the distance to the loop's end - see `curia help
progress`. Where jev is on, a last line gives this month's judgments: how
many, how many the cache answered, how many failed, and the cost, from
brain/jev/calls.log.""",

    "progress": """\
A seat's long run, read-only, waking by waking: when each session woke
and on which account, how long it ran and what it cost (its end line in
sessions.log), how the board stood when it woke and when it handed off
(workable = ready + in progress + blocked by other beads, which is what
`launch --everything` works down to zero; human = waiting on a person),
how it ended (handed-off, limited, or its exit status), and the first
line of what its handoff put under "For the principal". Then the board as
it stands now, read the way the launcher reads it (origin's export after
a fetch, else the file on disk), with the distance to the loop's end and
who the human-waiting beads wait on; and the totals for the window:
wakings, handoffs, spend, minutes, beads closed and filed.

The window is the latest launcher's sessions (those sharing its pid on
their start lines), so a --loop or --everything night is one view;
--since widens or narrows it. The board is the repo the latest session
started in, or --repo. The board columns come from the `board` lines the
launcher writes at each waking; before those exist they are rebuilt from
the export's own timestamps (created, started, closed) with labels and
dependencies as they stand now, and the output says which it did. A `?`
on either side of a workable column is a side with no reading: a session
still awake, or a night that ended before the board was read again.

--html draws the same as one self-contained page: workable and
human-waiting beads over time, the distance to done, and the wakings with
each handoff's line for the principal in full. That page is the only
thing this command writes; without --html nothing is touched. It reads no
API, wakes no seat, and never changes the board.""",

    "hook": """\
What a seat session's Claude Code hooks run. `launch`, `run` and
`dispatch` pass them to claude with --settings, so no account's
settings.json is edited and a session started any other way carries none.
The mechanism's hooks are below; the estate adds its own fences in
curia/hooks.json, in the shape of a settings file's "hooks" block, and
they ride along in every seat session. Outside a seat (no CURIA_SEAT, no
CURIA_ESTATE) every hook here does nothing.

precompact: fires on a manual /compact inside a seat. The house rule is
hand off, don't compact, so the hook refuses (exit 2) and tells the seat
to /handoff. To allow one anyway, touch COMPACT-OK in the seat's directory
from outside the session; the hook consumes it and lets that one /compact
through. Auto-compaction is never blocked.

usage: the status line. Claude Code hands it what it measures - the
account's rate-limit windows (five-hour, seven-day, a spend limit where
one applies), each with a used percentage and a reset time, the session's
cost, and how full the session's context window is - and the hook writes
them to curia-usage.json in the account's config dir and to
curia-usage/<session id>.json, then prints one line for the bar. That
file is what rotation, the shift hook and `status` read. Nothing here
asks the API anything.

shift: at the end of every turn. Past handoff_at on the account's fullest
window, past shift_hours awake (estate.toml), past context_at on this
session's context window (default 60 percent: a long context costs more
every turn and hands off worse, so the note is written while the seat is
still sharp; 0 turns it off), past the deadline if --until is set
(wrap_minutes before), or past the budget if --budget is set
(budget_wrap_at percent), it tells the seat once to hand off - exit 2, so
the seat carries on with that as its orders - and leaves SHIFT-OVER so it
is said once; handoff --done or a launch clears it. The environment carries
CURIA_UNTIL (ISO UTC), CURIA_BUDGET (USD), and CURIA_SPENT (recorded USD
so far this night). Headless, or when Claude is already continuing because
of a stop hook, it says nothing.

limited: StopFailure on a rate limit, when a turn dies because the account
is out of budget. Marks the account limited - until the retry-after the
error names, else the measured window's reset, else the reset time in the
banner, else a few hours; a rate limit naming no reset at all is taken as
transient and marked for ten minutes - and leaves the seat a LIMITED
marker carrying the session id and transcript path. `launch` watches for
that marker, ends the session and relaunches (`curia help launch`). In a
headless run the marker is named for the run (LIMITED-<session id>), so a
session of the seat awake alongside is not ended for a review's limit;
`run` reads it before it reads the banner, and takes its reset time. A
payload the hook cannot read marks nothing.

release-guard: a PreToolUse fence on Bash, and the worked example of one.
A `git push` naming a repo's release branch, where that branch is not
also the integration branch, is refused (exit 2) with the rule: open a PR
and let the gate land it. It matches the command text, so a push with no
refspec while standing on the release branch gets past it; the rule, not
the hook, covers that. The estate template wires it in hooks.json.

record-guard: a PreToolUse fence on Edit and Write (MultiEdit and
NotebookEdit too), enforcing the house rule never to falsify the record.
An edit to what the mechanism writes or the reader alone archives - a
seat's sessions.log, assignments.log, laurels, handoff history, journal
entries of other wakings, run logs, the offices' reports under brain/,
the fences log - is refused (exit 2) with where the fact belongs instead:
the handoff or journal, a postmortem bead, a line for the principal. The
one exception is the journal entry of this headless waking (CURIA_JOURNAL
in the run's environment), which is the seat's to write. It sees the
file-editing tools only, so a shell redirection into the record gets past
it; the rule covers that. The estate template wires it in hooks.json.

Every refusal by a mechanism fence is one line in the estate's
brain/fences.log: when, which seat, which fence, what was refused. An
estate's own fence appends a line of the same shape (`<ISO time>
seat=<seat> fence=<name> <what>`). `curia lictor` reads it back and says
which fences are idle and which are busy; `check` notes when hooks.json
declares more than fences_max. A fence is distrust written down, and the
log is the one fact that says which of them earn their keep.""",

    "help": """\
With no argument, the overview: vocabulary, the daily rhythm, every
command. With a command, that command's page in full.""",
}

